(2335368, 322573, 50674, '<td valign="top">I stayed at Hotel Stein a few years ago, this looks very much like the view from the top floor, the veranda. It was closed at the time, but I went out there anyway.<br/>Anyway. This is nicely caught, you have many different things there all in one picture. Different light, different kind of buildings, variety of colours and then the mountains in the background, covered with snow and almost spring in the foreground. I am not sure, but is seems to tilt a tiny little bit to the left, but as I say, not sure.<br/>This must be my longest comment ever, but it was so nice to see Salzburg again :-) Good luck in the challenge, well done.</td>', 'I stayed at Hotel Stein a few years ago, this looks very much like the view from the top floor, the veranda. It was closed at the time, but I went out there anyway.Anyway. This is nicely caught, you have many different things there all in one picture. Different light, different kind of buildings, variety of colours and then the mountains in the background, covered with snow and almost spring in the foreground. I am not sure, but is seems to tilt a tiny little bit to the left, but as I say, not sure.This must be my longest comment ever, but it was so nice to see Salzburg again :-) Good luck in the challenge, well done.', '2006-04-22 18:28:25.000000', NULL, 1),
(2335369, 320412, 48245, '<td valign="top">errr</td>', 'errr', '2006-04-22 18:31:07.000000', NULL, 1),
(2335426, 320906, 30364, '<td valign="top">Great face.  Interesting that his positioning, facing out of the frame but looking back into it, seems to make the portrait at once more candid and more dignified than would have any of the alternative ''conventional'' compositions.  By that I mean that he appears to be exerting some control over the image; he''s more than just a photogenic prop - he''s a participant in the photograph (it''s not all about the photographer).  Maybe it also helps to connect him a little better and more convincingly with his environment, too.  Very nice!  9.</td>', 'Great face.  Interesting that his positioning, facing out of the frame but looking back into it, seems to make the portrait at once more candid and more dignified than would have any of the alternative ''conventional'' compositions.  By that I mean that he appears to be exerting some control over the image; he''s more than just a photogenic prop - he''s a participant in the photograph (it''s not all about the photographer).  Maybe it also helps to connect him a little better and more convincingly with his environment, too.  Very nice!  9.', '2006-04-22 19:04:52.000000', NULL, 1),
(2335436, 322913, 30364, '<td valign="top">It has a very pleasing touch of madness, or at least whimsy ... something to do with the severely symmetrical composition and the slightly manic, obsessive expression on the man''s face (as if he spends hours daily in a frenetic search for new words).   9.</td>', 'It has a very pleasing touch of madness, or at least whimsy ... something to do with the severely symmetrical composition and the slightly manic, obsessive expression on the man''s face (as if he spends hours daily in a frenetic search for new words).   9.', '2006-04-22 19:09:01.000000', NULL, 1),
